-- title:
--   Incomparable branch ideals at a crossing point
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime with $5 \le p$, let $\mathfrak{X}$ be a Deligne–Rapoport model package `DRModelPackage p` for the integral two-chart model `DRModel p` over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathbb{Z}$, let $O$ be a discrete valuation ring (a commutative domain) whose maximal ideal is the ideal generated by the image of $p$, let $\kappa$ be an algebraically closed field of characteristic $p$ and $t \colon O \to \kappa$ a ring homomorphism. Work in the base change $\mathfrak{X}_O = \operatorname{pullback}$ of `DRModel.toBase p` along $\operatorname{Spec}(\mathbb{Z} \to O)$. Let $n$ be a point of the fibre product of the two component maps $\mathfrak{X}.\mathrm{compInf}\,\kappa$ and $\mathfrak{X}.\mathrm{compZero}\,\kappa$, and let $x_n = \mathfrak{X}.\mathrm{crossingPt}\,O\,\kappa\,t\,n$ be its image in $\mathfrak{X}_O$ under the first projection followed by $\mathfrak{X}.\mathrm{compInf}\,\kappa$ followed by the base-change map attached to $t$. Assume the two distinguished points $\xi_\infty = \mathfrak{X}.\xi\mathrm{inf}\,O\,\kappa\,t$ and $\xi_0 = \mathfrak{X}.\xi\mathrm{zero}\,O\,\kappa\,t$ both specialise to $x_n$. For each such specialisation, the branch ideal in the stalk $\mathcal{O}_{\mathfrak{X}_O, x_n}$ is the preimage of the maximal ideal of the stalk at the generisation under the specialisation map of stalks. The conclusion is that neither of the two branch ideals, that of $\xi_0 \rightsquigarrow x_n$ and that of $\xi_\infty \rightsquigarrow x_n$, is contained in the other.
--
--   This records that the two analytic branches through a crossing point of the special fibre of the Deligne–Rapoport model base changed to $O$ are genuinely distinct in the stalk: the local ring at a crossing point is not dominated by a single branch. It is used in the analysis of the local structure at crossing points, for the lower bound on the Krull dimension of such a stalk, for the production of oriented crossing charts, and in the chart presentation of stalks of the resolved model.
